# Free-flying satellite: a floating (6-DOF) main bus carrying two solar
# panel arms and an antenna boom -- the standard floating-base example
# (RBDA ch. 9, sec 9.4). The 'floating' joint is a genuine 6-DOF joint
# with quaternion orientation, so there is no gimbal lock at any attitude.
# Coordinate layout (nq = 10, nv = 9):
#   q[0..3]  bus orientation, unit quaternion (w,x,y,z); normalized on input
#   q[4..6]  bus position in base coordinates
#   q[7..9]  appendage joint angles
#   qd[0..5] bus spatial velocity in its own frame (wx,wy,wz,vx,vy,vz)
#   qd[6..8] appendage joint rates
# tau and out_qdd use the qd layout. Thruster force enters through fext on
# the bus. Under gravity alone the whole assembly is in free fall: at
# identity attitude qdd[5] = -9.81 (body-frame linear z), all appendage
# accelerations zero.

gravity 0 0 -9.81

body                    # 0: main bus, free-flying; thruster fext here
parent -1
joint floating
tree 0 0 1
mass 100
cg 0 0.02 0.05
inertia 10 12 8 0.5 0.2 0.3
fext 1

body                    # 1: left solar panel arm
parent 0
joint revolute y
tree 0.6 0 0
mass 5
cg 0.5 0 0
inertia 0.1 0.45 0.5 0 0 0
fext 1

body                    # 2: right solar panel arm
parent 0
joint revolute y
tree -0.6 0 0
mass 5
cg -0.5 0 0
inertia 0.1 0.45 0.5 0 0 0

body                    # 3: antenna boom
parent 0
joint revolute z
tree 0 0.3 0.4
mass 1
cg 0 0.15 0
inertia 0.01 0.002 0.01 0 0 0
